WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.320
If you've been working as an instructional 
designer or researching the field,  

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.960
then you've probably realized by now 
that a lot of the training out there  

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:15.120
is either boring or downright ineffective and 
unnecessary and the biggest reason for this  

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.880
is because a lot of learning designers and 
training teams just completely skip analysis  

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.640
and design training because someone says that they 
need it. So in this video we're going to look at  

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.840
the five different types of analysis that you can 
use on your next instructional design project.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.760
Alright, so, analysis is the name of the game 
today and I'm not even kidding when I say that  

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.200
like if we as instructional designers could get 
this right and had the opportunities to conduct  

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:59.920
these analyses we could probably contribute like 
a massive amount to the global GDP just because of  

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:06.080
how much more we would help be helping people and 
companies that we support. So again we're looking  

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.720
at five of like the main types of analysis. These 
are the traditional types of analysis that you  

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.160
would learn about in a master's program but there 
are some more streamlined approaches to creating  

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.280
efficient and effective training which 
we'll talk about later in the video.  

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.640
So for now, we're just going to take a look 
at these five different types of analysis.  

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.600
You could take a look at these if you want but we 
are going to get to each and every one of them.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:37.040
Now, needs assessment. This is probably the one 
that you've already heard of the main question  

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.720
we're asking and answering here is why aren't 
people performing at the desired level right  

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.880
and we can explore this by interviewing people by 
observing them by talking to the star performers  

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.320
and also the people who are really struggling. 
There are many different approaches to this. It  

00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:00.560
might just be asking the client or the internal 
stakeholder a series of "Why?" questions until  

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.320
you feel like you've really drilled down 
into what's causing the problem. Sometimes  

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.360
the client or internal stakeholder won't know and 
you will need to speak to the actual audience that  

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.040
you support and I'll do a little disclaimer here 
and say like that is what analysis is all about.  

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.160
We want to make sure that we are supporting our 
audiences with what they actually need to perform  

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.920
better instead of assuming oh it's a course on 
effective communication or it's a course on this.  

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.600
We want to really speak to our people, observe 
our people, empathize with them, and get to know  

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.480
how are we really going to support this person 
in the best way possible so they can perform  

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.120
better on the job? That's really the overarching 
question and theme when it comes to analysis.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.080
So, why aren't people performing at the desired 
level and what will help them perform at the  

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.880
desired level? So, not just why aren't they doing 
it right but also what will help them do it better  

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.920
and in a lot of cases when we take the time to 
ask these questions and talk to our people and  

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:04.080
see where they're having trouble we might find 
these problems are being caused by environmental  

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.440
factors like the technology they're using or 
the reward systems that are in place. We might  

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.960
be getting rewarded for doing the wrong thing or 
we might not be getting properly incentivized to  

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.000
do the right thing so there are a lot of things 
at play that may not mean oh we should design  

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.360
training for this specific audience maybe you're 
designing training for their managers or maybe  

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:33.520
you're investing in new technology or new systems 
but we can't throw training at every problem. Now,  

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:39.520
training can help when there's a gap in skill or 
knowledge. So that's the main problem we solve  

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.240
as learning designers, right? When people are 
at point A we need to get them to point B. They  

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:49.440
currently know how to do this. We need them 
to know how to do either that same thing more  

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.760
efficiently or some other things so that they 
can properly contribute to the organization. 

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.680
So if you're going to take away one thing from 
this it's that we only want to design training  

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.480
when it's solving a gap in skill or knowledge 
and we don't want to assume that the audience  

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.040
has a gap in skill or knowledge and we don't want 
to assume that that gap is what's causing the  

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.840
problem. Usually, it's a combination of multiple 
things. There's a gap in knowledge or skill,  

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.160
there are environmental factors, there 
are inappropriate reward systems in place.  

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.400
It's usually a combination we just want to be 
clear about exactly how we're helping solve  

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:29.280
that problem as learning designers. So again 
this is like the first and most important  

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.680
form of analysis that you can conduct. It tells 
you whether or not you even need training, so if  

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:41.280
we're experts in designing learning experiences 
you can see why this piece is so important.  

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.160
We don't just you know when you're a hammer like 
everything is a nail like that kind of saying like  

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.080
we don't want to look at every problem as a 
learning problem. We want to be very picky  

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:56.320
and do this diagnosis to determine can my efforts 
here where I'm going to spend many many hours  

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.440
you know speaking to subject matter experts, 
writing content, developing that content,  

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.920
we only want to use this skill set and use 
these hours if it's actually going to make  

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.800
an impact to make a difference. And again 
this is in an ideal world the reality is  

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.240
there are a lot of cultural issues working against 
instructional designers where we're kind of not  

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:22.160
being incentivized to put in this upfront time but 
for those of you who are newer or fell into your  

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:27.040
ID role and didn't learn much about analysis, this 
is kind of like the proper approach and the proper  

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.000
the way to determine if training is even 
necessary. And sometimes on some teams that  

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:38.000
are learning consultants and they do just this or 
performance consultancy may also refer to them as  

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.840
and then when they do decide or determine that yes 
training will help solve this problem in this way  

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.760
that's when we would turn it over to an 
instructional designer. So it's okay to  

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.360
just design the content or design the practice 
activities if there is someone like upstream who  

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.160
is doing that hard upfront analysis work and it 
doesn't have to be that hard or that difficult  

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:04.320
but it does take a little bit of time to 
dive deeper into these problems. But this  

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.800
right here is the most important one we can do 
a whole video on this, just let me know in the  

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.440
comments if you'd like to. We also maybe could do 
like some some live events where we do like some  

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.400
mock needs assessments like we could have some fun 
with this but it is such an important skill set  

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.680
and it really does not have to be very difficult, 
it's just digging deeper asking questions.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:32.720
So the next one is job task analysis. So you also 
may see this abbreviated as JTA. My impression is  

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.680
that this was much more popular back in the day 
when I was doing my master's program one of our  

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:44.400
professors who was like a veteran practitioner he 
used to say I paid for my first house doing JTAs,  

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:50.240
so he would he would just get hired by companies 
to come in and conduct this and then the output  

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:56.320
here is a list of all of the tasks that we should 
prioritize training for. So let's talk a bit about  

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.200
what it actually is. So this is where we would 
interview or observe people to determine which  

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:07.440
tasks they actually have to perform. Some of 
you may already be familiar with action mapping  

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:14.640
that is kind this definitely is kind of wrapped 
into that in some ways but the way we were taught  

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:20.720
this in our master's program is that this was like 
very like an exhaustive inventory of every single  

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:26.640
task that someone would perform in the context of 
achieving a given goal right? So we need someone  

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:33.040
to operate this machine, so we would document like 
every single thing like walk up to the machine,  

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:39.600
like press this button to turn the machine on, 
check this mirror, check this thing. Like a lot of  

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:44.480
the times if you're just sitting down with a star 
performer or one of these employees and asking  

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.040
tell me every single thing you need to do to 
have to get this done it's only natural that  

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:54.400
they're going to skip some steps because the 
experts and again this is who you'd want to  

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.840
interview for this, we want to interview the 
people who are performing at the desired level  

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:02.560
but they might be doing things and not even 
realizing that they're doing things and that's  

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:07.360
where as an instructional designer it's important 
to to be very meticulous and drive very deep into  

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:14.080
these tasks. So they might skip steps so you would 
ask do you do anything in between those steps  

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:21.520
or observation can help a lot to you there's a 
form of this called cognitive job task analysis  

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:27.600
where you even talk about what decisions and what 
processes the employee is going through like in  

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:32.720
their head so look into those. Again I get the 
sense that they're a bit more traditional. I have  

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:39.440
seen some opportunities come up in the freelancer 
contract space like over the past four years  

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.160
I've maybe seen like one or two people ask for 
someone who's really good at job task analysis  

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.920
so I don't know if this is a dying art or what and 
again there are more modern approaches that let us  

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:54.960
focus on the right actions it's called action 
mapping. In particular if you've been following  

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.920
me for a while you know I love that process and 
we've done some content on it so we have a whole  

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:03.680
action mapping playlist if you want to 
see a more modern streamlined approach  

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:10.720
to focus on which actions people need to perform. 
So once you have this exhaustive list of actions  

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:17.200
for this job task analysis, we would ask the 
employee to rate each and every single one of  

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:24.160
those tasks in terms of frequency, difficulty, 
and importance. So how often do you do this?  

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:29.200
How difficult is it to do this? and How 
important is it that you do this? For  

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:34.000
another way to say that is maybe like How severe 
are the consequences if you do not do this?  

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:40.800
and the idea is that you would do this with maybe 
it's just one star employee but probably more like  

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:46.080
three to five so you get a pretty exhaust you 
know a pretty exhaustive rating system on these  

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:52.720
tasks from multiple employees and then you as the 
instructional designer you can identify which one  

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:59.040
of those tasks are the most important. So if we 
see a pretty decent list of tasks where it's very  

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:03.360
frequent, very difficult, and very important, 
clearly that's where we're going to get the best  

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:10.560
return on investment when we foc- you know when 
it comes to focusing our training efforts. So  

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:14.960
look into this if this approach sounds appealing 
to you but again action mapping I would probably  

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:19.200
recommend as the modern equivalent and action 
mapping wraps up a little bit of everything like  

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.840
it wraps up some needs assessment it wraps up some 
of this but this may help just from like seeing  

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:29.680
where our field has come from and seeing like the 
proper traditional way to conduct these analyses

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:35.600
Alright, so, learner analysis. So this is a 
very important one too that is often overlooked.  

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:42.240
You can go to different levels of depth with 
this but what it is really about is we want to  

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:50.240
understand our audience's needs. Basically, we 
want to be able to empathize with them really,  

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:55.680
really well so that we can wrap that into 
our design decisions for the solutions we're  

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:01.440
designing and developing. So mostly this will 
look like interviews and surveys so we're talking  

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:07.120
to our people we want to determine what their 
preferences are in relation to technology and  

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:15.680
delivery mechanism and we also want to know where 
they're at with the task at hand or the topic at  

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:19.920
hand. We want to know their background knowledge. 
So, how comfortable are they with this content?  

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:24.720
So again what's their comfort with the tech, 
with the topic? What are their preferences for  

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:30.960
art style, modality? So you know do they really 
prefer videos? Have they taken e-learning before  

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:38.640
and they're comfortable using those navigations? 
