00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:50.160
maybe we'll just use like plain text like open 
menu for example. So when you know more about  

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:55.600
your audience, you can design more for that 
person and that's probably clear from this  

00:12:55.600 --> 00:13:02.320
but the end result is a learning experience that's 
more tailored, appealing, and effective. So get to  

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:05.920
know your people, right? A lot of the times 
you just get stuck working with our subject  

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:11.440
matter experts or our clients and we can complete 
entire projects without once hearing from someone  

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:17.040
in our actual audience who we're supporting 
and yeah sometimes we you know sometimes we  

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:23.280
ask for feedback so we get some survey information 
but that's that's not really what this is right  

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:28.240
we're not asking for specific feedback about 
a learning experience. We want to learn more  

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:32.800
about who that person is, what their daily 
lives are like, and just get a really strong  

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:37.440
sense of that. So our learning solution will 
really hit the mark when it gets delivered.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:45.200
Okay, so these two are maybe not as popular. I 
don't see these really talked about a lot but  

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:51.280
I remember when I learned about instructional 
context analysis and job context or performance  

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:56.160
context analysis which is the next one I was like 
that makes so much sense and I can see how if you  

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:03.360
skip these it can lead to some very big problems. 
So essentially we're this is the question we're  

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:08.560
answering: what context will people be in 
when they engage with the learning experience?  

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:13.120
So where am I going to be? Am I going to be in 
front of a desktop computer in an office building  

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:17.760
and I'm going to be clicking through an 
e-learning experience? Or am I going to be  

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:25.280
sitting in a conference room listening to 
a facilitator with five of my co-workers?  

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:29.600
So, again, think about when the people are 
actually taking you know going through the  

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:34.400
learning experience that you design whether that's 
face to face or e-learning, what is the context in  

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:40.240
that environment? So for face-to-face learning 
experiences, we want to look at that context and  

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:44.800
figure out how many seats do we have available to 
us, right? Do we have enough seats for the amount  

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:50.960
of people that we need to accommodate? Do we have 
a projector if we need to show slides? Is there a  

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:56.960
computer there to even load up a PowerPoint? Like 
we basically want to see what do we have available  

00:14:56.960 --> 00:15:02.800
to us in this space or in this context so that we 
can make sure we design accordingly. We don't want  

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:08.720
to design a PowerPoint for the facilitator to use 
if we don't even have a computer or a projector  

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:13.120
in that room. It just doesn't make much sense and 
that's where we can lead to a lot of wasted money  

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:19.120
for e-learning. It's still very, very important 
because we want to know what devices are people  

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:25.520
using, right? Are they going to be on cell phones 
or are they going to be on computers? What is the  

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:31.440
screen size? Should we be designing a nice full 
screen wide screen type of experience or maybe  

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:37.600
everyone maybe all the computers on the or in the 
office have like a vertical orientation? What's  

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:42.480
the processing power, right? What is the internet 
speed? Like we want to we want to figure out is  

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:48.880
it okay to include some very high quality media 
that needs to get downloaded onto their computer  

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:56.640
because if we don't have the right hardware to 
run that then it's going to be pretty ineffective  

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:01.440
like the training isn't even going to work or 
it's going to be very frustrating and people might  

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:06.640
just dip out of it. Sound cards. So there's a 
big example with this one. So if we're going to  

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:11.440
include sound in our learning experience, we want 
to make sure that the computers opening them up  

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:17.760
have sound cards and maybe this is standard and is 
default these days but there was a story that one  

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:23.040
of our professors told us in the master's program 
and it was just about this training program where  

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:28.960
a company invested millions into this really 
like slick high production value like narrated  

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:33.280
e-learning and then when it came time to deliver 
it to the audience they found out that none of  

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:38.800
the computers in the office had sound cars so 
they would have to re-outfit every single device  

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:47.040
in the space to experience that learning project. 
So you can see these things are important maybe we  

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:53.600
can take sound cards for granted these days but 
I hope this just illustrates the point that we  

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:59.120
need to be careful with this context analysis. 
We need to make sure that we're designing for  

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:02.480
things that are available in the spaces where 
people will be engaging with the training.

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:08.240
And then performance context is very similar 
but instead of you know which context will  

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:12.960
people be in when they go through the training 
experience, we want to know what context people  

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:18.319
will be in when they have to apply what they've 
learned. So what is it like on the actual job,  

00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:23.359
right? We want to make sure that the solution 
we're designing works for that context. Maybe  

00:17:23.359 --> 00:17:27.919
people have desktop computers back in the office 
where they go for training but if these people  

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:32.000
are working in the field and they're working 
out of their cars and out of clients houses  

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:37.680
or businesses maybe all they have with them is a 
cell phone, so if we design this nice wide screen  

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:42.960
learning experience that we want people 
to be able to access like from the job  

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:48.960
it's not going to be a very good time if they're 
trying to refer back to that on a mobile device.  

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:54.720
And similarly like maybe we think oh it will be 
great to design this job aid that lists all these  

00:17:54.720 --> 00:18:00.000
like important communication principles but then 
if the person on the job is constantly in front  

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.160
of clients or in front of people it might not 
make sense for them to pull out like a paper  

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:09.920
job aid and look at that while they're having a 
conversation. So you can see and then similarly if  

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:14.640
I'm working from a computer we want to know that 
the specs of that computer, what it can handle.  

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:19.520
Maybe if we're designing tools we want to make 
sure that it works for the technology that our  

